userStories.description:
**Goal** — Implement the repository's all-words, any-word, substring search order without changing its existing filters or response shape.

**Motivation** — `websearch_to_tsquery` ANDs every word.

**Approach** — Target: the probe also returns the query's text form, written without a subquery alias so the existing probe assertion (`numnode(websearch_to_tsquery(`) stays true: `SELECT numnode(websearch_to_tsquery('english', $text)) AS "lexemes", websearch_to_tsquery('english', $text)::text AS "query"` (both through the existing `tsQuery(text)` helper). The search order becomes:
1. All words: `findRankedByText` with the existing `websearch_to_tsquery` expression. Rows found are returned.
2. Any word: only when step 1 found nothing **and** the probe's `query` contains ` & ` **and** contains no `!`. The same ranked query runs with the expression `replace(websearch_to_tsquery('english', text)::text, ' & ', ' | ')::tsquery`. It keeps every filter (scope, `deletedAt`, status, TTL bound, kind, subject prefix), the paging, the explicit `orderBy` when given, and otherwise `ts_rank` order. Rows found are returned.
3. Substring: `findByContains`, as today, with the text term matching `subject`, `predicate`, `object` **or `description`**, case-insensitive (#456).
A probe row without `query` (or a `null` one) skips step 2.

Worked skeleton for the repository change (shape only; names other than the public method are the implementer's):
```ts
async findByProjectMemory(query, page) {
  const text = searchText(query.text);
  if (text !== undefined) {
    const probe = await this.searchProbe(text);            // { lexemes, query }
    if (probe.lexemes > 0) {
      const all = await this.findRankedByText(query, tsQuery(text), page);
      if (all.total > 0) return all;
      if (anyWordAllowed(probe.query)) {
        const any = await this.findRankedByText(query, anyWordTsQuery(text), page);
        if (any.total > 0) return any;
      }
    }
  }
  return this.findByContains(query, page);
}
const anyWordTsQuery = (text: string): Prisma.Sql =>
  Prisma.sql`replace(${tsQuery(text)}::text, ' & ', ' | ')::tsquery`;
const anyWordAllowed = (q: string | null | undefined): boolean =>
  typeof q === 'string' && q.includes(' & ') && !q.includes('!');
```
The `::tsquery` cast keeps the lexemes as `websearch_to_tsquery` already stemmed them; `to_tsquery` would stem them a second time. A phrase (`<->`) inside the text stays a phrase in the any-word pass.

The any-word pass is plain Postgres full-text search over the existing generated `searchVector` column and its index. No migration, no embeddings, no synonym dictionary. `ts_rank` over an OR query scores a row higher when it holds more of the query's lexemes, so the note that matches most words of a question comes first.

**Scope** — In: change PrismaMemoryItemRepository and its existing unit harness. Out: the real Postgres, REST and koda_memory_search quality proofs belong to US-005; tool write/schema changes belong to US-001/US-002/US-003. The search response does not report which pass (all words, any word or substring) produced the rows. `ContextBuilderService`'s memory read, which passes no text, is not changed. Search gains no synonyms, embeddings, typo tolerance or language other than the `english` text-search configuration, and the `searchVector` column and its index are not changed (no migration). The any-word pass is not run for a text containing a negated word.

**Interface** — Keep the public signature unchanged:
```ts
async findByProjectMemory(query: ProjectMemoryQuery, page: IPageOption): Promise<IPageResult<MemoryItem>>
```
Private ranked lookup receives the query expression as Prisma.Sql so both passes reuse the same filter/order/page/hydration logic. Probe returns lexemes and optional/null query rather than a boolean. Retain bound english::regconfig/text values via tsQuery. A ranked pass wins on total > 0, not records.length, including beyond-last-page requests. Preserve readScope/scopeWhereSql authorized owner union and project-only default, explicit status versus default active/TTL behavior, deletion/kind/literal starts_with filters, existing orderBy variants and unique tiebreakers. Substring's text OR must stay separate from the TTL OR and scope AND.

**Modifies** — `apps/api/src/memory/memory-item-repository.spec.ts` — the substring cases (the textTerm helper and the falls-back-to-contains case) assert the text term is an OR over exactly subject, predicate and object; the term now also matches description, so those expected objects gain the description contains term with mode insensitive. The ranked() helper gains an optional probe query value (default none, so existing cases keep a probe row without query). The probe assertion (probe SQL contains numnode(websearch_to_tsquery( ) stays true by the Target's no-alias probe form.

**Verification** — Extend the existing unit harness first for probe/ordered raw calls, both ranked outcomes and fallback, keeping existing scope/filter cases. Probe + all-word ids/count is three raw calls; eligible any-word ids is raw call four and its count is five. On any-word success restore hydration to SQL id order and do not call Prisma memoryItem.count. On both failures use the four-field substring OR. Use tdd-simple for this bounded repository change: preserve the existing readScope/scopeWhereSql implementations and filter predicates rather than changing authorization rules. Existing scope/filter unit assertions remain regression anchors for both uses of the same ranked-query builder.
